{"cuisine/djiboutian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/djiboutian-cuisine","n":"Djiboutian cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Djibouti","intro":[["Djiboutian cuisine is a mixture of Somali, Afar, Yemeni, and French cuisine, with some additional South Asian (especially Indian) culinary influences."],["Local dishes are commonly prepared using a variety of Middle Eastern spices, ranging from saffron to cinnamon. Grilled Yemeni fish, opened in half and often cooked in tandoori-style ovens, are a local delicacy. Spicy dishes come in many variations, from the traditional fah-fah or soupe djiboutienne (spicy boiled beef soup), to the yetakelt wet (spicy mixed vegetable stew)."],["Xalwo or halva is a popular confection eaten during festive occasions,such as Eid celebrations or wedding receptions. Halva is made from sugar, corn starch, cardamom powder, nutmeg powder and ghee. Peanuts are sometimes added to enhance texture and flavor."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"djiboutian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Djiboutian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]}],"cont":"African cuisine"},"cuisine/vietnamese-chinese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/vietnamese-chinese-cuisine","n":"Vietnamese Chinese cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Diaspora cuisine in Vietnam","intro":[],"src":null,"wiki":"Vietnamese Chinese cuisine","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"vietnamese-chinese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vietnamese_Chinese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/kapampangan-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/kapampangan-cuisine","n":"Kapampangan cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Philippines","intro":[["Kapampangan cuisine differed noticeably from other groups in the Philippines. The Kapampangan kitchen is the biggest and most widely used room in the traditional Kapampangan household."],["When the Philippines was under Spanish rule, Spanish friars and sailors taught Kapampangans the basics of Spanish cooking. The Kapampangans were able to produce a unique blend that surprised the Spanish palate. Soon, Spanish friars and government officials were entertaining foreign guests at the expense of Kapampangan households. In the late 18th century, the Arnedo clan of Apalit were commissioned by the colonial government to entertain foreign dignitaries, including a Cambodian prince and a Russian archduke. Kapampangans were given the task of creating the meals and menus that were served in the proclamation of the First Philippine Republic in Malolos, Bulacan."],["Some popular Kapampangan dishes include sisig, morcon, menudo, caldereta, estofado, embotido, asado, lengua, lechon, chicharon, afritada, sipo egg, bringhi (paella), tabang talangka (crab meat), the \"tocino\" or pindang including pindang damulag or carabao’s meat tocino and their native version of the longganisa. A unique Kapampangan dish that is well enjoyed by other ethnic groups is nasing biringyi (chicken saffron rice). Since nasing biringyi is so difficult to prepare, this unique Kapampangan dish can only be enjoyed during fiestas in Pampanga.  It is comparable to Malaysian nasi biryani."],["Some unusual Kapampangan dishes include balo balo or burung bulig (mudfish fermented in rice) from Candaba, betute tugak (stuffed frogs) from Mexico (Pampanga) and Magalang, adobung kamaru (mole crickets sautéed in vinegar and garlic), calderetang barag (spicy monitor lizard stew), kubang asu (sweet and spicy dog stew) from Macabebe, and tidtad itik (duck stewed in blood) from Masantol."],["Other heritage dishes include tidtad (dinuguan, a stew of fresh pig's blood, cooked with pork and liver), begukan (ribs and liempo cooked in pork & shrimp paste), hornong pistu (baked pork meatloaf of chorizo, ham and cheese), asadong dila (ox tongue with sauce and castañas) and kilayin (chopped liver and lungs)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"kapampangan-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kapampangan_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Kapampangan Kitchen","ref":"site/kapampangankitchen-blogspot-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"kapampangankitchen.blogspot.com","note":"Blog dedicated to Kapampangan home recipes from Pampanga.","url":"https://kapampangankitchen.blogspot.com","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Atching Lillian's Heirloom Recipes","ref":"cookbook/atching-lillian-s-heirloom-recipes","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Lillian Mercado Lising Borromeo","note":"Heirloom recipes from a noted Pampanga culinary historian and cook.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Cabalen","dish/cabalen"],["Tibok-tibok","dish/tibok-tibok"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/palembang-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/palembang-cuisine","n":"Palembang cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Padang food","cuisine/padang-food"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Indonesia","intro":[["Palembangese cuisine is the cuisine of the Palembangese people of the city of Palembang in the South Sumatra province of Indonesia. It is the second most well-known cuisine from Sumatra after Padang."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"palembang-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palembang_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Padang food","cuisine/padang-food"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":3,"links":[["Malbi","dish/malbi"],["Pempek","dish/pempek"],["Tekwan","dish/tekwan"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Sweet soy sauce","ingredient/sweet-soy-sauce"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["Flavor enhancer","ingredient/flavor-enhancer"],["Noodles","ingredient/noodles"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Tamarind","ingredient/tamarind"],["Tapioca","ingredient/tapioca"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Celery","ingredient/celery"],["Edible mushrooms","ingredient/edible-mushrooms"],["Egg white","ingredient/egg-white"],["Fish cake","ingredient/fish-cake"],["Fishcake","ingredient/fishcake"],["Sago","ingredient/sago"],["Scallion","ingredient/scallion"],["Shallot","ingredient/shallot"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"],["Vermicelli","ingredient/vermicelli"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Padang food","cuisine/padang-food"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/aragonese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/aragonese-cuisine","n":"Aragonese cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Spain","intro":[["The Aragonese cuisine includes several typical dishes and ingredients of the local cuisine of Aragon, a community in Spain."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"aragonese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aragonese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":3,"links":[["Cañada bread","dish/canada-bread"],["Chistorra","dish/chistorra"],["Regañao","dish/reganao"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Paprika","ingredient/paprika"],["Parsley","ingr