ada-de-santa-luzia"],["Pão na chapa","dish/pao-na-chapa"],["Pie","dish/pie"],["Sobá","dish/soba"],["Xis (sandwich)","dish/xis-sandwich"]]}]},"dish/funchoza":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/funchoza","n":"Funchoza","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Koryo-saram cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Funchoza or salat funchoza (салат фунчоза; 살라트 푼초자; lit. 'cellophane noodle salad') is a dish in Koryo-saram cuisine. It is a variant of the Korean dish japchae, created by the Koryo-saram: a group of the Korean diaspora of the former Soviet Union."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-funchoza"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Funchoza"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Koryo-saram cuisine","cuisine/koryo-saram-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Cellophane noodles","ingredient/cellophane-noodles"],["Glass noodles","ingredient/glass-noodles"]]},{"h":"More from Koryo-saram cuisine","links":[["Pukjai","dish/pukjai"],["Siryak-tyamuri","dish/siryak-tyamuri"]]}]},"ingredient/sesame-oil":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/sesame-oil","n":"Sesame oil","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Sesame oil is an edible vegetable oil derived from sesame seeds.  The oil is one of the earliest-known crop-based oils. Worldwide mass modern production is limited due to the inefficient manual harvesting process required to extract the oil. Oil made from raw seeds, which may or may not be cold-pressed, is used as a cooking oil. Oil made from toasted seeds is used for its distinctive nutty aroma and taste, although it may be unsuitable for frying, which makes it taste burnt and bitter."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-sesame-oil"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sesame_oil"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Cantonese cuisine","cuisine/cantonese-cuisine",[["Har gow","dish/har-gow"]]],["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine",[["Yakgwa","dish/yakgwa"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bamboo shoot","ingredient/bamboo-shoot"],["Cornstarch","ingredient/cornstarch"],["Scallions","ingredient/scallions"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"],["Honey","ingredient/honey"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}]},"dish/hokka-hokka-tei":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/hokka-hokka-tei","n":"Hokka Hokka Tei","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Japanese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Hokka Hokka Tei is a bento take-out chain with over 2,000 franchises and company-owned branches throughout Japan.  It offers a variety of dishes, generally over rice, at relatively low price.  Unlike competitors such as Yoshinoya beef-bowl and the various cheap curry establishments, there are no chairs or counters for inside-dining."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-hokka-hokka-tei"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hokka_Hokka_Tei"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Just One Cookbook","ref":"site/justonecookbook-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"justonecookbook.com","note":"Namiko Chen's long-running blog and videos on Japanese home cooking.","url":"https://justonecookbook.com","suggested":true},{"name":"RecipeTin Japan","ref":"site/japan-recipetineats-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"japan.recipetineats.com","note":"Japanese home recipes from Yumiko, a Japan-born cook, sister site to RecipeTin Eats.","url":"https://japan.recipetineats.com/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Japanese cuisine","links":[["Benihana","dish/benihana"],["Bergh–Stoutenburgh House","dish/bergh-stoutenburgh-house"],["Bincho","dish/bincho"],["Boxer Ramen","dish/boxer-ramen"],["Cagen","dish/cagen"],["Chikuwa","dish/chikuwa"],["Curry bread","dish/curry-